{
  // The house's light switches. `HOUSE-00384`, the first rows of `interactables.json`; the other
  // eleven behaviour classes of §50.4 come later and this file grows into §53's 640.
  //
  // §53: "switch plates are interactables placed at 1.20 m on the correct side of each doorway",
  // and a multi-gang plate offers one action per gang. So a plate is one row with one action per
  // group it controls, and the **state field is named after the group** -- `state.LG_L0_HALL_MAIN`
  // -- which is what makes a three-way pair expressible: §53's "a group with two switch props" is
  // two plates whose gang names the same group, and §53's "one bit per group" is that field.
  //
  // Placement is derived: the plate goes beside the room's entry doorway -- a door into a
  // corridor first, then any way in -- 0.30 m clear of the opening on the side with more wall
  // beside it, 0.06 m inside the room from the wall plane, at 1.20 m above that cell's floor.
  // Rule 11 then proves every one of them is reachable from a standing eye position, which is the
  // point of putting them in the data rather than in a level editor.
  //
  // Two groups are switched from the NEXT room, because their own is too low to stand up in: the
  // garage loft (1.40 m under its rafters) is switched at the bottom of the ladder and the
  // under-stair cupboard (1.75 m) from the hall. Fifteen groups have no plate at all, which §53
  // lists: the table and floor lamps are switched at the lamp, the refrigerator's interior light
  // by its door, and the porch lanterns and street lights by a dusk sensor.
  "schema": "cna-house/interactables/1",
  "interactables": [
