    // ================================================================ the windows
    // `HOUSE-00402`. 54 rows: every window that opens. §12.6's twelve fixed lights are not here --
    // the two picture windows, the two gable louvres, the transom, the two sidelights and the
    // sunroom's five glazed panels -- because a light that does not open is geometry and nothing
    // else.
    //
    // 54 and not the 62 §12.6 designed: that split was never reachable from §13's per-room column,
    // which is what `HOUSE-00376` recorded when it authored the 66 windows. §12.6 carries the
    // measured breakdown now, and this is the openable half of it.
    //
    // Named `WIN_<cell>_<facade><n>`, which is §65.6's own `WIN_L1_MASTER_N2` shape and cannot
    // collide with the OPENING ids, which are `WIN_<cell>_<n>`. The facade letter earns its place:
    // "the second north window of bedroom 2" is a thing a person can find in a house of 66 of
    // them.
    //
    // `openFraction` is continuous, like a door's: a sash is half up, a hopper is on its catch,
    // and §65.6 starts `WIN_L1_MASTER_N2` at 0.50 so weather audio through an opening is testable
    // from the first minute.
    //
    // The focus is the latch, at the sash's own height rather than a door handle's 1.05 m. Rule 11
    // proves every one reachable from somewhere a player can stand -- which for a basement hopper
    // at -0.45 means from the basement floor and not from the yard.
